WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.047
Over nine chapters, we've walked through the whole system: students, teachers, time, places, what's taught, the machinery, the money, and the scoreboard.

2
00:00:09.047 --> 00:00:12.832
Now the question that matters most: what do we do with all this?

3
00:00:12.832 --> 00:00:18.128
Some people look at a system this complex and say it should be blown up and rebuilt.

4
00:00:18.128 --> 00:00:21.595
Replace districts with networks of independent schools.

5
00:00:21.595 --> 00:00:25.756
Give every family a funding account to spend wherever they choose.

6
00:00:25.756 --> 00:00:38.681
Let technology replace much of classroom teaching, through personalized online platforms like Khan Academy, or through all-online charter schools built around the idea that kids can move at their own pace.

7
00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:42.779
These ideas have real appeal, and pieces of them have been tried.

8
00:00:42.779 --> 00:00:50.849
The evidence suggests disruption can work in specific places when it's carefully managed, and can do real harm when it's rushed.

9
00:00:50.849 --> 00:00:52.425
Schools are not startups.

10
00:00:52.425 --> 00:00:54.316
Kids only get one third grade.

11
00:00:54.316 --> 00:00:57.027
Others argue the fix is simple: more money.

12
00:00:57.027 --> 00:01:04.088
It's worth asking what well-funded schools actually do with their resources, and it helps to put a number on it.

13
00:01:04.088 --> 00:01:11.528
California public schools spend somewhere in the range of eighteen to nineteen thousand dollars per student each year.

14
00:01:11.528 --> 00:01:15.563
Many elite private schools spend fifty thousand dollars or more.

15
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:29.559
Wealthy districts and private schools tend to buy smaller classes, more counselors, richer arts and sports programs, better-paid and more experienced teachers, and lots of individual attention for students who fall behind.

16
00:01:29.559 --> 00:01:36.935
Those are good things, and research suggests money spent this way helps, especially for students who start with less.

17
00:01:36.935 --> 00:01:48.095
It's also fair to note that private schools can choose which students they admit, so some of their strong results come from who attends, not just from what the extra money buys.

18
00:01:48.095 --> 00:01:53.643
Still others argue the opposite: that tight budgets force schools to focus and innovate.

19
00:01:53.643 --> 00:01:57.804
There are schools that do remarkable things with modest resources.

20
00:01:57.804 --> 00:02:02.217
But there's little evidence that scarcity itself makes schools better.

21
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:07.639
More often, lean budgets mean cutting the very supports that help struggling students.

22
00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:13.187
The honest middle ground is that money matters, and how it's spent matters just as much.

23
00:02:13.187 --> 00:02:16.336
So how does change actually happen in education?

24
00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:19.748
Slowly, and usually through a combination of forces.

25
00:02:19.748 --> 00:02:21.257
Research builds a case.

26
00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:23.487
Advocates and parents push for it.

27
00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:26.899
Lawmakers or voters change the rules or the funding.

28
00:02:26.899 --> 00:02:33.329
And then, the hardest part, thousands of educators have to actually change what they do every day.

29
00:02:33.329 --> 00:02:38.971
There's a simple test that can help you tell whether any of this will actually matter.

30
00:02:38.971 --> 00:02:49.140
It's sometimes called the Ed100 Razor: does the change put different people in the room, or does it change how kids and teachers actually spend their time?

31
00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:56.816
If a reform doesn't do one of those two things, it probably won't change much, no matter how good it sounds on paper.

32
00:02:56.816 --> 00:03:07.117
Big reforms often fail not because the idea was wrong, but because the implementation was rushed, underfunded, or ignored the people who had to carry it out.

33
00:03:07.117 --> 00:03:13.743
Lasting change tends to come from steady, focused improvement over years, not dramatic announcements.

34
00:03:13.743 --> 00:03:15.174
Which brings us to you.

35
00:03:15.174 --> 00:03:19.341
You don't need to be an expert or an official to make a difference.

36
00:03:19.341 --> 00:03:21.270
Here are a few places to start.

37
00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.318
Learn how your own school and district are doing.

38
00:03:24.318 --> 00:03:31.658
Look them up on the California School Dashboard, and look at the results for each student group, not just the average.

39
00:03:31.658 --> 00:03:32.155
Show up.

40
00:03:32.155 --> 00:03:36.572
Attend a school site council, a PTA meeting, or a school board meeting.

41
00:03:36.572 --> 00:03:43.165
Most boards have a public comment period, and a thoughtful, specific comment from a parent carries weight.

42
00:03:43.165 --> 00:03:44.720
Get involved in the LCAP.

43
00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:48.763
Every district must gather community input on its plan each year.

44
00:03:48.763 --> 00:03:54.175
Ask how the extra money for high-need students is being used, and whether it's working.

45
00:03:54.175 --> 00:03:59.711
Vote in school board elections, and look at local bond and parcel tax measures carefully.

46
00:03:59.711 --> 00:04:04.750
These are decided by small numbers of voters, which means your vote goes further.

47
00:04:04.750 --> 00:04:07.176
And support the adults in the building.

48
00:04:07.176 --> 00:04:11.157
Teachers, aides, counselors, and principals are doing hard work.

49
00:04:11.157 --> 00:04:15.884
Volunteering, thanking them, and backing good ideas makes a real difference.

50
00:04:15.884 --> 00:04:20.248
If you want to keep learning, there are excellent resources.

51
00:04:20.248 --> 00:04:23.304
EdSource covers California education news.

52
00:04:23.304 --> 00:04:29.123
CalMatters, a nonprofit newsroom, covers California policy, including education.

53
00:04:29.123 --> 00:04:37.488
The Public Policy Institute of California and Policy Analysis for California Education publish accessible research.

54
00:04:37.488 --> 00:04:42.871
The California Department of Education's website has data on every school.

55
00:04:42.871 --> 00:04:54.000
The Legislative Analyst's Office, the state's nonpartisan budget and policy analysis office, digs into how education dollars are spent and what they buy.

56
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:06.221
And the free Ed100 course at ed100.org, which inspired the structure of this series, goes deeper on every topic we've covered, lesson by lesson, in English and Spanish.

57
00:05:06.221 --> 00:05:12.766
Every student deserves a great education, and a great education system doesn't happen by accident.

58
00:05:12.766 --> 00:05:17.374
It happens because people understand how it works and decide to help.

59
00:05:17.374 --> 00:05:19.044
Now go make a difference.
